TASK: Wire the kit models into the rendered town (plan: v1-playtest-slice)

WHAT SHIPPED
- src/game/town/townLayout.ts — pure, three.js-free plan: ground quads, one road
  model per road tile with the yaw for its derived shape, houses cycling the
  building registry and capped to their lot, props keyed to their kind.
- src/game/town/townRenderer.ts — async mount: instantiates each placement from
  the model library, seats every model on the ground by measured bounding box,
  and fits oversized kit buildings to their lot. Owns teardown.
- src/game/assets/modelRegistry.ts — role-named URLs (roads/houses/nature/props)
  with `?url` imports, so only models actually mounted are built and precached
  (19 emitted; the rest of the 292 committed kit models cost nothing).
- src/game/town/townBuilder.ts (+ test) deleted — the placeholder geometry the
  kit models replace.
- main.ts mounts the town and warms the loader behind the first paint.

ORIENTATION (measured, not assumed)
scripts/blender-analyze-kit.py now reports, per palette swatch, which
bounding-box edges its faces reach; that list is a piece's orientation:
- road-straight: asphalt reaches west+east → band runs east-west, 0.60 wide.
- road-bend-square: asphalt reaches west+south → elbow at yaw 0 is the (5,0)
  corner; kerb wraps the outer north/east sides.
- road-intersection: west+east+south → east-west through with a south stem.
- road-end: east only → opens east, so its yaws sit a quarter turn off the
  town's `+z`-facing helper.
Positive rotation.y was verified against three.js itself (yaw +pi/2 carries
model-east onto world-north), which is what the bend/corner yaws depend on.

SEATING / GROUND
- Roads: base z = 0, asphalt z = +0.01, kerb top z = +0.02 → no lift, tileSize 1
  needs no rescaling; the 1x1 square bend is used for ring corners, so no map
  reshaping for the 2x2 road-curve.
- Ground tiles stay lawn-coloured quads (City Kit Roads' tile-low is pavement,
  and the town wants grass under its lots); one shared plane, laid flat per mesh.
- Kit pieces the spec has no art for: the crashable hydrant becomes a traffic
  cone (props/spec gap recorded in the plan as outstanding).

